    "['storm-control broadcast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast level 1500', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control broadcast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast level 1500\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast storm-control-action shutdown\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast recovery-timeout 300\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper uses a storm control profile (default in this case) where thresholds and actions are configured separately\\n2. `1500` represents 1.5 Mbps (1.5m in Cisco) as the rising threshold\\n3. `300` represents the recovery timeout (3 minutes/3m in Cisco)\\n4. The action is set to shutdown when the threshold is exceeded\\n5. Juniper doesn't have an explicit falling threshold like Cisco, but uses the same rising threshold value for hysteresis\", 'trans_template': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast level 1500'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast storm-control-action shutdown', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control broadcast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast level 1500\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast storm-control-action shutdown\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast recovery-timeout 300\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper uses a storm control profile (default in this case) where thresholds and actions are configured separately\\n2. `1500` represents 1.5 Mbps (1.5m in Cisco) as the rising threshold\\n3. `300` represents the recovery timeout (3 minutes/3m in Cisco)\\n4. The action is set to shutdown when the threshold is exceeded\\n5. Juniper doesn't have an explicit falling threshold like Cisco, but uses the same rising threshold value for hysteresis\", 'trans_template': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast storm-control-action shutdown'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast recovery-timeout 300', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control broadcast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast level 1500\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast storm-control-action shutdown\\nset forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast recovery-timeout 300\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Juniper uses a storm control profile (default in this case) where thresholds and actions are configured separately\\n2. `1500` represents 1.5 Mbps (1.5m in Cisco) as the rising threshold\\n3. `300` represents the recovery timeout (3 minutes/3m in Cisco)\\n4. The action is set to shutdown when the threshold is exceeded\\n5. Juniper doesn't have an explicit falling threshold like Cisco, but uses the same rising threshold value for hysteresis\", 'trans_template': 'set forwarding-options storm-control-profiles default broadcast recovery-timeout 300'}]]": 1,
    "['storm-control multicast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m threshold 3m', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control multicast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m threshold 3m\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `ethernet-switching-options` for switch-specific configurations\\n- `storm-control` is the equivalent feature\\n- `interface all` applies to all interfaces (you could specify a specific interface instead)\\n- `multicast` matches the traffic type\\n- `level 1.5m` sets the rising threshold (1.5 Mbps)\\n- `threshold 3m` sets the falling threshold (3 Mbps)\", 'trans_template': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m threshold 3m'}]]": 1,
    "['storm-control unicast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth-level 1500', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco `storm-control unicast level 1.5m 3m` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth-level 1500\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast no-broadcast-level 3000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses separate commands to set the rising threshold (1500 kbps) and falling threshold (3000 kbps)\\n2. The 'all' can be replaced with a specific interface name if needed\\n3. The values are in kbps (1500 kbps = 1.5 Mbps, 3000 kbps = 3 Mbps)\\n4. Juniper doesn't have an exact 1:1 mapping for Cisco's storm control, but this is the closest equivalent functionality\", 'trans_template': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth-level 1500'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast no-broadcast-level 3000', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for the Cisco `storm-control unicast level 1.5m 3m` configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth-level 1500\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast no-broadcast-level 3000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses separate commands to set the rising threshold (1500 kbps) and falling threshold (3000 kbps)\\n2. The 'all' can be replaced with a specific interface name if needed\\n3. The values are in kbps (1500 kbps = 1.5 Mbps, 3000 kbps = 3 Mbps)\\n4. Juniper doesn't have an exact 1:1 mapping for Cisco's storm control, but this is the closest equivalent functionality\", 'trans_template': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all unicast no-broadcast-level 3000'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\ndelete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable\\n##\\n\\nFor Juniper devices, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is to remove the \"disable\" statement from the interface configuration. The Juniper command structure is different - instead of explicitly enabling the interface, you delete the disabling configuration. \\n\\nNote: The exact interface name might differ slightly between platforms (e.g., \"ge-1/0/0\" instead of \"GigabitEthernet1/0/0\" in Juniper), but I maintained the original interface name format as requested.', 'trans_template': 'delete interfaces GigabitEthernet1/0/0 disable'}]]": 1,
    "['storm-control broadcast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 1500', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control broadcast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 1500\\nset protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 3000 threshold\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., ge-0/0/0)\\n2. The Cisco command uses 1.5m (1.5 Mbps) and 3m (3 Mbps) as levels, which are converted to 1500 and 3000 kbps respectively in Juniper\\n3. Juniper separates the configuration into the base bandwidth limit and threshold values\\n4. You would need similar commands for multicast and unicast control as separate statements in Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 1500'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 3000 threshold', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control broadcast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 1500\\nset protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 3000 threshold\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. Replace `<interface-name>` with the actual interface name (e.g., ge-0/0/0)\\n2. The Cisco command uses 1.5m (1.5 Mbps) and 3m (3 Mbps) as levels, which are converted to 1500 and 3000 kbps respectively in Juniper\\n3. Juniper separates the configuration into the base bandwidth limit and threshold values\\n4. You would need similar commands for multicast and unicast control as separate statements in Juniper\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols storm-control interface <interface-name> broadcast bandwidth 3000 threshold'}]]": 1,
    "['storm-control multicast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m burst-size 3m', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `storm-control multicast level` command:\\n\\n##\\nset ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m burst-size 3m\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `ethernet-switching-options` hierarchy for storm control settings\\n- The command configures multicast storm control with:\\n  - `level 1.5m` as the threshold (equivalent to Cisco's first value)\\n  - `burst-size 3m` as the burst level (equivalent to Cisco's second value)\\n- The `interface all` applies it to all interfaces (in Cisco it was interface-specific)\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, you might need to apply this under specific interfaces if you don't want it global, similar to how Cisco does it per interface.\", 'trans_template': 'set ethernet-switching-options storm-control interface all multicast level 1.5m burst-size 3m'}]]": 1,
    "['storm-control unicast level [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 1500', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `storm-control unicast level 1.5m 3m`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 1500\\nset protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 3000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses bandwidth in kbps (not packets per second like Cisco)\\n2. The first value (1.5m) becomes 1500 (1.5 Mbps)\\n3. The second value (3m) becomes 3000 (3 Mbps)\\n4. \"unicast\" is directly supported as a storm control type\\n5. You would need to replace \"all\" with the specific interface name(s) in a real deployment', 'trans_template': 'set protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 1500'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 3000', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command for `storm-control unicast level 1.5m 3m`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 1500\\nset protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 3000\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses bandwidth in kbps (not packets per second like Cisco)\\n2. The first value (1.5m) becomes 1500 (1.5 Mbps)\\n3. The second value (3m) becomes 3000 (3 Mbps)\\n4. \"unicast\" is directly supported as a storm control type\\n5. You would need to replace \"all\" with the specific interface name(s) in a real deployment', 'trans_template': 'set protocols storm-control interface all unicast bandwidth 3000'}]]": 1,
